      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, the SettingWithCopyWarning! It's like a mischievous goblin that loves to pop up when you're trying to do something clever with your DataFrame. This warning is your notebook's way of saying, \"Hey, you're trying to change a slice of your DataFrame, but you're not making a copy of it first. That's a bit risky, you know?\"\n\nTo avoid this goblin, you need to make sure you're working with a copy of the slice, not the original DataFrame. You can do this by using the `.copy()` method. So, if you're filtering your DataFrame and then adding a new column, you should do something like this:\n\n```python\ndf_filtered = df[df['column_name'] == 'value'].copy()\ndf_filtered['new_column'] = 'new_value'\n```\n\nThis way, you're giving the goblin a copy to play with, and it won't pop up to cause trouble. Now, you can add your new column to the filtered DataFrame without any SettingWithCopyWarning goblins popping up. Happy coding!",
